// A table that is full grows or stops the compiler — it never drops what it was handed.
//
// The rule in `packages/wacc/README.md`, "A full table grows or traps", and the helper for it. A fixed-size table
// whose guard was `if (n >= xs.len()) { return; }` dropped the entry past it without a word, and every such drop
// found so far was a wrong answer somewhere else: a type an importer never declared (`issues/lang/0468d`), an import
// edge resolved by guesswork, a local that reset on resume, two symbols merged into one member. When growing is
// awkward — several parallel arrays, a table read by index from many places — write through this instead:
//
//     xs[slotOrTrap(n, xs.len())] = x;
//
// A bare `trap`, because the bootstrap's compiler cannot build one with a message; a crash names the place it was
// hit, which is what matters, and it is never the wrong answer a dropped entry is.

/** `used`, when there is room for one more at that index; a trap when the table is full. */
export i32 slotOrTrap(i32 used, i32 capacity) {
  if (used >= capacity) { trap; }
  return used;
}
